             i.e. the STRING "False", which Odoo's Boolean converter coerces to
             True — so the item the comment above says was retired has been on the
             SETUP rail ever since. `eval` is what makes a boolean a boolean.
             Sequence 70 is kept rather than parked in W18's 900 band: nothing
             else in SETUP claims it, and W18 leaves non-colliding retirements
             where they are. -->
        <field name="active" eval="False"/>
    </record>

    <!-- ============ Workforce ============
         The Option-A rail, reached in P1b. ACTIVE order (each item's record
         lives with the module that owns the surface, so this file only holds
         the ones pb_sidebar itself declares):

           10 Today · 20 Schedule · 30 Time · 40 Time Off · 50 Overtime
           60 Trips · 70 Approvals · 80 Shift Templates

         Everything else is RETIRED, never deleted: `active` false takes it off
         the rail, and the record moves into the 900+ band because a deactivated
         item still OCCUPIES its sequence for W8's uniqueness rule (W18) — the
         moment an admin re-enables one, a duplicate would matter.

         WORKFORCE P7 QUALIFIED THAT RULE. "Retire, never delete" is only worth
         anything while re-enabling the item would still WORK — the point of
         keeping the row is that the surface is one click from coming back. P7
         was the "later cleanup with its own doAction-caller audit" this comment
         used to promise, and it deleted the Gen-0 cockpits themselves: the
         Workforce Dashboard, Live Attendance, Timecards and Overtime Rules
         boards, their JS, their CSS and their client actions. Their four rail
         records went with them, because a retired item pointing at a missing
         action is not a reversible decision, it is a button that answers an
         admin's click with nothing — and `pb_today/tests/test_sweep.py`
         asserts exactly that (every retired item's `action_xmlid` must still
         resolve).

         The rows are dropped from the database by
         `pb_hr_workforce/migrations/19.0.4.14.0/post-migrate.py` — removing a
         record from a data file does not delete it, and the module that
         deleted the actions is the one that owes the cleanup. Every retired
         item still HERE points at a live action. -->

    <!-- RENAMED by Workforce P1b: "Shift Roster" -> "Schedule". Label and
         sequence only — the action, the cockpit and the persona are untouched;
         P2 reskins the surface itself. The rail now reads as the manager's
         week (Today · Schedule · Time · …) rather than as a list of tables. -->
    <!-- REPOINTED by Workforce P2: the rail's Schedule entry now opens the
         `pb_schedule` cockpit. `action_xmlid` is a plain Char resolved lazily,
         so this file does not gain a dependency on pb_schedule and the record
         is harmless on a database where that module is not installed.
         `match_action_tags` used to keep the LEGACY `shift_planning_grid` tag
         listed as well, so the Gen-0 grid lit this item rather than none.
         Workforce P7 deleted that cockpit and its client action, so the tag can
         never be the active one again and the list names only what exists. -->
    <!-- RETIRED by Workforce P3a: Schedule is now a LENS of the Mission Control
         shell (pb_mission mounts the same component with embedded="true", W17).
         Both actions — the P2 cockpit and the legacy grid — survive untouched;
         only the rail entry goes, into the 900 band (W18) because the shell's
         single "Workforce" item takes 10 and every retired sequence still has
         to be unique. -->
    <record id="item_wf_roster" model="pb.sidebar.item">
        <field name="section_id" ref="sec_workforce"/><field name="name">Schedule</field>
        <field name="icon">calendar</field><field name="sequence">907</field>
        <field name="active" eval="False"/>
        <field name="action_xmlid">pb_schedule.action_pb_schedule</field>
        <field name="action_tag">pb_schedule</field>
        <field name="match_action_tags">pb_schedule</field>
        <field name="groups_id" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('hr_attendance.group_hr_attendance_officer')])]"/>
    </record>
    <!-- RETIRED by Workforce P2: folded into Schedule's templates drawer
         (pb_schedule). A shift library is configuration you touch while you are
         building a roster, not a destination of its own — and the rail lands on
         its final Option-A shape of SEVEN items.

         It moves into the 900+ retired band, because a deactivated item still
         OCCUPIES its sequence for W8's uniqueness rule (W18) — and 80 is now
         free for whatever P3 puts there. The native action survives untouched;
         the drawer opens its form, and P2 gives that list the search view it
         never had (pb_schedule/views/shift_template_views.xml). -->
    <record id="item_wf_templates" model="pb.sidebar.item">
        <field name="section_id" ref="sec_workforce"/><field name="name">Shift Templates</field>
        <field name="icon">layers</field><field name="sequence">905</field>
        <field name="action_xmlid">pb_hr_workforce.action_shift_template</field>
        <field name="active" eval="False"/>
    </record>
    <!-- RELOCATED by Workforce P1b: Payroll Report is a PAYROLL surface that
         happened to live under Workforce — salary aggregates by department, for
         the payroll persona. It moves to the Pay Run section, after Payslips,
         and keeps P0's gate.

         Gated to match its legacy menu twin (pb_hr_workforce/views/menu_views.xml
         menu_payroll_report, groups="om_hr_payroll.group_hr_payroll_user"). The
         sidebar entry shipped ungated, so it exposed salary aggregates to any
         Workforce persona (W8). -->
    <record id="item_wf_payroll_report" model="pb.sidebar.item">
